  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 320 AM EST Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry, seasonable, and pleasant through the middle of \nthe week. \n\nA dry cold front is currently passing through the area this morning. \nThe antecedent and post-frontal mid and upper levels are very dry, \nso there aren't even any clouds along or behind the front. Despite \nthe clear skies, a light SW wind is keeping the low-levels just \nmixed enough to prevent appreciable radiational cooling. \nTemperatures are generally in the upper 40s to around 50 F and \nmorning lows should just be a few degrees cooler. Rural/sheltered \nlocations could briefly dip into the upper 30s. For today, sunny \nskies and seasonable temperatures in the 60s are forecast. Breezy \nWNW winds are also expected in the well-mixed, post-frontal airmass, \nparticularly in the late morning and early afternoon. Wind gusts up \nto 25 mph or so are possible and could approach 30 mph on the lower \nMD Eastern Shore. Low temperatures tonight likely drop in the 30s \nfor most of the area, except in the 40s at the immediate coastline. \nThere is some potential for a light freeze in rural interior VA \n(particularly near the NC/VA border). Frost is also possible in \nthese areas and could eventually need a targeted Frost Advisory\nfor areas still in the growing season. \n\nOtherwise, a dry and pleasant stretch of weather is expected through \nthe middle of this week as high pressure meanders south and \nsouthwest of the area. Modest height rises on Tuesday allow for high \ntemperatures a degree or two warmer compared to today, \ngenerally in the upper 60s to around 70 F. Similarly mild on \nWednesday with even some lower 70s creeping into the forecast \nacross interior portions of eastern VA and NE NC. Expect a \ngradual increase in clouds late in the day Wednesday as upper-\nlevel moisture begins to overspread the area ahead of developing\nsfc low pressure in the Midwest. Overnight lows in the 30s \ninland and 40s near the coast Tuesday night and in the 40s area \nwide Wednesday night. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A strong cold front brings chances for rain Thursday-\nFriday, though rain amounts should remain on the lighter side. Much \ncooler temperatures follow behind the front. \n\nThe latest model guidance continues to signal a major pattern change \nat the end of the week. An anomalous 500mb trough develops and digs \ninto the eastern half of the country Thursday into Friday, with a \nsprawling surface low initially developing in the Midwest and OH \nRiver Valley area Thursday. There is some disagreement across the \ndeterministic and ensemble guidance beyond Thursday, but most data \nshows a Miller B-type evolution, with a secondary sfc low then \ndeveloping offshore of the area Thursday night as the upper trough \npivots toward the Mid-Atlantic. Regardless of the specifics, this \nwould mean that much cooler, below average temperatures are \neventually on the way for Friday and the weekend. Highs in the 50s \nare expected Friday into early next week with Saturday currently \nlooking like the chilliest day. Overnight lows look to be around \nfreezing each night away from the immediate coast and even could \nbriefly dip into the upper 20s. This system also appears likely to \nbring some rain chances to the area, particularly later Thursday \ninto early Friday. The jury is still out on how much rain we can get \nout of this system. The ECMWF ensemble remains the most optimistic \nfor a half inch of total QPF, generally 20-40% and 40-60% for the \nsouthern and northern halves of the area, respectively. The GEFS and \nGEPS continue to show little to no probs in this category. In \ngeneral, areas N/NE of I-64 have the best shot at appreciable \nrainfall during this time. Drier behind this system for the\nweekend.",
